This package is a new extraction of Linux getrandom ABI flag, length and
readiness policy designed for TheKernel and distributed under Apache-2.0. It
is not a port of any upstream file: no Linux source file was translated,
adapted, or restructured here.

Linux sources were consulted to understand observable flag acceptance, the
`GRND_INSECURE`/`GRND_RANDOM` conflict rule, the `crng_ready()` gate and its
`-EAGAIN`/blocking split, and the `import_ubuf()` length clamp. No other
upstream was consulted for this package.

Copied text: Linux C is quoted verbatim in Rust doc comments as the
observable-semantics authority. Measured against the Linux 7.2.3 tree
(whitespace-normalised lines of >= 40 characters inside fenced code blocks,
matched line for line): 4 lines across 3 blocks, all in `src/lib.rs`; at the
inclusive >= 25-character threshold 6 lines across the same 3 blocks. The
hunks are `drivers/char/random.c:1385-1393`, `:1395-1401` and
`lib/iov_iter.c:1445-1450`. Two of the three blocks are dominated by short
lines and are effectively disclosed by the `:1385-1393` and `:1395-1401`
markers, which sit above them; every block now carries an inline marker line,
"Excerpt: Linux v7.2.3 <file>:<lines> - GPL-2.0-only,
(C) The Linux Kernel Authors", in addition to the citation its surrounding
prose already gave.

Those fragments are GPL-2.0-only, (C) The Linux Kernel Authors. They are
comment text, not code: no Linux code is linked, assembled, or compiled into
this package, and the Apache-2.0 grant above does not extend to the quoted
text. Nothing else in this package reproduces upstream source text. See
`docs/upstream-provenance.md` for the full per-file index.
